 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+### Rotating the password
+
+To change the password without signing everyone out, pass a list to `password`. The first entry seals every session, and the others only open sessions sealed before the rotation:
+
+```js
+import { useSession } from "h3";
+
+app.use(async (event) => {
+  const session = await useSession(event, {
+    password: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD, process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD],
+  });
+
+  return session.data;
+});
+```
+
+Each password in the list must follow the same rule as a single one: at least 32 random characters. Keep these behaviors in mind:
+
+- **Order matters.** Keep the current password first. An older password in the first position seals all new sessions with it.
+- **Migration happens on read.** A session opened with an older password is resealed with the first one when it is read from the cookie and then written or slid under `idleTimeout`. Sessions that are never read again keep the older seal until they expire or the old password leaves the list.
+- **Header sessions do not migrate.** A session sent in the `x-{name}-session` header is never resealed, so it keeps working with the older password until it expires.
+- **Removing a password signs out its sessions.** Once a password is removed from the list, every session sealed with it is treated as unknown.
+- **Each password costs CPU.** A request whose cookie no password opens costs one PBKDF2 derivation per password, so keep the list to two or three entries.
+
